{
 "concept": "Final plan: the judges' winner (\"gameplay\", 2 votes of 3) with every named flaw fixed and the best grafts of the two others. A cross-shaped country cottage, not a box. A two-storey main body runs east-west (x0..5, z0..1, ridge X). A two-storey cross wing (x2..3, z-1..2, ridge Z, same eave, so a true cross gable) gives the front gable with a full-width vestibule, front door and two-cell porch on the SOUTH, and the back stair wing with the back door on the NORTH. WEST: a two-cell glass veranda on a brick base leaning on the living room gable. EAST: a single-storey garage with its own south-facing gable (ref_03) pushed one cell forward, so front gable and garage gable frame a small front court facing the truck. BACK: a small single-storey gabled scullery ell (x4..5, z2) holds the stone cellar stair. It replaces the winner's lean-to: same cells, but a plain GABLE prism, so no second roof primitive and no half-gable cheek. Ground floor: hall spine x=3 from front door to back door; living room west behind ONE INT_DOOR, with the veranda entered at its far end; dining room on the street side and grandmother's kitchen at the back as in Pierre's drawing, forming a 6-cell loop around a wall (hall, dining, kitchen); garage reached through one INT_DOOR from the dining room, plus a garage rear door that closes an outdoor loop. The wooden stair is its own walled stair hall with only the foot open onto \"the knot\" (3,2): stair foot west, cellar stairhead east, back door north, corridor south. Upstairs keeps Pierre's 1er etage: grandmother's bedroom west, bathroom north-east, guest room south-east (linked by a second door for an upstairs loop), a lived-in sewing attic in the front gable behind the two feature windows, a trunk store in the north wing, a railed gallery over the stairwell. Cellar: 8 cells, main cellar with boiler corner plus a workshop. 24 + 16 + 8 = 48 cells.",
